        "file": "lib/eal/linux/eal_memory.c",
        "start_line": 1905,
        "end_line": 1909,
        "side": "new",
        "severity": "low",
        "category": "bug",
        "title": "Secondary fails to attach when primary ran with --no-huge (VA range unset)",
        "explanation": "eal_dynmem_memseg_lists_init returns 0 early when no_hugetlbfs is set, so the primary never fills in mcfg->mem_va_addr, mem_va_len or mem_va_page_sz. The no-huge memseg list is created later by the legacy init path, with its own VA reservation. A 64-bit secondary whose synced legacy_mem is false always takes memseg_secondary_init_dynmem, sees mem_va_addr == 0 and fails with \"Missing shared dynamic memory VA range\". Before this change, the secondary attached to that memseg list and reserved its VA through eal_memseg_list_alloc. Any setup that runs a secondary against a --no-huge primary now fails at init.",
        "suggestion": "If the shared VA range is not set (or the primary was in no-huge mode), use the per-list legacy attach path: call memseg_secondary_init_legacy() instead of returning an error. Another option is to reserve per list only those memseg lists that fall outside [mem_va_addr, mem_va_addr + mem_va_len).",
        "evidence": "if (mcfg->mem_va_addr == 0 || mcfg->mem_va_len == 0 ||",
        "confidence": 0.45,
        "id": "11677f76f1c8",
